{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh's direction, 2026-10-11 22:10 Eastern: write a design note on what a hosted dap at dap.generalbusiness.ai requires beyond the domain and the production register, where a person registers with an email address and a key and claims a room on one open register. The note is a design, not an implementation; it proposes and names decisions for Hugh.\n\nFacts found on 2026-10-11 that the note starts from. Identity is a key; an open register lets any key claim a repository and become that room's admin; ARTIFACTS_CONFIG pins one register per deployment. Founding is unguarded today: anyone can POST /v1/scopes to install a register or found a directory under any definition (docs/scopes.md: nothing checks the authority to install). Only join, enrol and sessions have serving limits (packages/scope/src/limits.ts, sessions.ts). The page offers a claim only to a key already in a room (allowedClaim in packages/page/src/main.ts); there is no first-claim route for a new key. The CLI learns its register and base URL only from install, and nothing is published to npm. Bounds in force: 100,000 entries per scope, 64 KB per edit, 1 GB per Artifacts repository; Artifacts operations are billed from 2026-10-14.\n\nThe note covers, in order: (1) admission at founding as serving protection outside the record, in the manner of the join limits: register installs only by the operator key, a short-lived ticket required on a claim and on a directory founding, Turnstile on the start page, and a zone rate-limiting rule for POSTs; (2) the door: email verification by magic link (Cloudflare Email Sending from the zone is the first choice; name the alternative and its cost), the ticket's form and lifetime, and the operator's table of email, key ID, rooms and start time, kept outside any scope; (3) suspension: a list of directories whose acts the Worker refuses while reads stay open, so a person keeps artroom clone and the history routes as their export, and how a suspended room's repository is later deleted; (4) first-run: a start route on the page for a new key under the service register, what it shows afterwards (invite link, CLI config, agent connection), and a CLI connect command or pasted config; publishing the CLI to npm; (5) caps set from measurements: operations per claim and per edit on the Artifacts host, measured before any per-person cap is chosen, with the bounds above restated; (6) operations: a landing page at the root instead of 404, terms, privacy and an abuse contact, Workers logs with one alert, session-secret rotation, and Durable Object point-in-time recovery as the backup story. The GitHub host stays off for hosted users because it creates repositories under our organization with our token; say what a person's own GitHub host would need.\n\nKeep commercial terms out of the note and the workroom: no pricing, tiers, trial length or hosting business shapes. Public words follow the presentation rules: no comparisons, no product names but GitHub.","body":{"conditions":"A design note under notes/ with the six sections above, each stating the requirement, the proposed form, what exists in the code today with file paths, and the decision Hugh must make where there is one. It names the serving-protection boundary for admission so nothing of it enters a scope's history. It contains no price, tier or trial term. It ends with an ordered list of implementation requests to file once Hugh decides, each with a builder estimate. Lands on main after review.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}